Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Monolith Forge**, a towering great forge whose
massive hammer pounds while its gear crown spins, as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel
model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque
voxel cells** and you **author its stamp and spin animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the forge is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the forge into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you
shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a pounding,
spinning forge needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving
each a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it;
each part is sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts
**empty**. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each
call **records only** — it appends the operation to that part's log and renders
nothing; run `voxel-anim render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews
(`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled scene. A pre-seeded `rig.json` holding the
required animation declarations is already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name base
voxel-anim fill-box --part base --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.)

Run `voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations —
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Run
`voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that
part's progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the
whole forge composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The
scene views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the hammer
rides centered in the forge's throat and the gear crown seats on its crest. Because
each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where
it belongs on the finished forge; the scene simply composes them there.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the forge up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a huge blocky masonry forge tower with a throat
up its center, a massive hammer riding in that throat, and a toothed gear crown
seated atop it. **Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the
`voxel-anim` animation subcommands: `hammer_stamp` (the great hammer dropping deep
down its throat and rising back to rest, landing with weight, not a weightless
slide) and `crown_spin` (the gear crown turning one steady full revolution and
looping seamlessly). Both are self-playing idles, so the forge cycles on its own
while the forge tower stays fixed. The animations are the fixed contract — produce
both, by those names; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. You may add
your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim
render` so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an
unrendered part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one
part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that
instant of an animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop:
the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
